<Econometrics title="Experiments in econometric reasoning" description="Three interactive investigations from The Econometric Argument, in which you change an assumption, watch the estimate move, and decide what the evidence can support. The page also links the Distribution Lab and the OLS assumptions lab." section="lab">
  <header class="ea-shell ea-page-intro"><p class="ea-kicker">Three econometrics experiments</p><h1>Identification and inference</h1><p class="ea-lead">Experiments on confounding, confidence-interval coverage, and sensitivity to parallel trends.</p><div class="ea-actions"><a href="#worlds">Identification</a><a href="#sampling">Uncertainty</a><a href="#design">Decision sensitivity</a><a href="#related">Related labs</a></div></header>
  <div class="ea-shell ea-experiments">
    <section class="ea-experiment" id="worlds"><div><p class="ea-kicker">Identification / Chapter 1</p><h2>Different causal effects, identical observations</h2><p class="ea-lead">Try to make the causal effect negative. Now look for something in the observed data that changed.</p><p>Changing the causal coefficient leaves the observed joint distribution of training and output unchanged.</p><a href="/econometrics/01-argument/">Read the argument and proof →</a></div><Worlds /></section>
    <section class="ea-experiment" id="sampling"><div><p class="ea-kicker">Uncertainty / Chapter 4</p><h2>Sample size and bias</h2><p class="ea-lead">First leave bias at zero and increase sample size. Then add a little bias and try again.</p><p>The first change buys precision. The second breaks the connection between the sampled population and the target. Notice what increasing sample size can and cannot repair.</p><a href="/econometrics/04-uncertainty/">Work through coverage and bias →</a></div><Sampling /></section>
    <section class="ea-experiment" id="design"><div><p class="ea-kicker">Identification to action / Chapters 8 &amp; 12</p><h2>Sensitivity to the trend assumption</h2><p class="ea-lead">A rollout appears to raise output. Allow the two groups to have different untreated trends. How far can they differ before buying the program becomes a judgment call?</p><p>At M = 3, the worst-case effect just covers the program cost, so no allowed effect loses money and training weakly dominates skipping. Any larger bound admits a loss. Defending a bound of 3 or less requires evidence about the untreated trends.</p><a href="/econometrics/08-panels/">Work through the identifying restriction →</a></div><Design /></section>
    <section class="ea-experiment" id="related" aria-labelledby="related-heading"><div><p class="ea-kicker">Distributions and regression assumptions / Chapters 2 to 4</p><h2 id="related-heading">Two related labs</h2><p class="ea-lead">Two larger laboratories elsewhere on this site support the probability and regression chapters.</p></div><div class="ea-prose"><p><a href="/teaching/distributions/">Distribution Lab</a>. Nineteen probability distributions with probabilities, quantiles, means, and variances; constructions of the t, chi-square, and F from their components; a central-limit construction; test power; and a simulated department whose data-generating processes are hidden. Its Code dialog writes the matching Python, R, Julia, and Stata. Chapter 2 links its bench and chapter 4 its constructions.</p><p><a href="/teaching/ols-assumptions/">OLS assumptions</a>. Seven Monte Carlo panels, one for each classical least-squares assumption, comparing coefficient bias, standard errors, and rejection rates as the assumption fails. Chapter 3 links the specification, exogeneity, and collinearity panels; chapter 4 links the independence, constant-variance, and normality panels.</p></div></section>
    <div class="ea-alert">These are deliberately small teaching models with known synthetic data-generating processes. They do not implement a production DML estimator, HonestDiD, or an evaluation of an actual training program. Each experiment includes its equations and limits.</div>
    <aside class="ea-help" aria-label="Tutoring" data-pagefind-ignore><p>Ian Helfrich tutors econometrics one-to-one, for students taking a course and for researchers designing a study. To work through these experiments in a session, start with a <a href="/book/#tutoring-intro" data-goatcounter-click="lab-tutoring-intro">20-minute intro call</a>; the <a href="/teaching/">teaching page</a> lists the subjects he covers and the kinds of support.</p></aside>
  </div>
</Econometrics>
